<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Banjul</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bathurst</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Banjul is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">The Gambia</a>, on St Mary's Island at the mouth of the Gambia river. It was founded as Bathurst in 1816 by the British, who wanted a base to suppress the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, and became the capital of the Gambia colony and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>. It was renamed Banjul in 1973, eight years after independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/banjul.jpg" alt="Banjul" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hannu Sääskilahti · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gambia,_Banjul,_Arch_22.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Banjul stands on St Mary's Island, also called Banjul Island, in the Gambia River where it enters the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a>. Bridges connect the island to the mainland, and ferries cross the river. There are several explanations of the name. One tradition says that Bandjougou, son of Barafin, came to the island fleeing the attacks of Soumaoro Kante on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manding">Manding</a> region, and that his name became Bandjoulou. Another derives it from Bang julo, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a> word for the rope fibre gathered on the island.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1651 the island was leased from the King of Kombo by the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> and Semigallia, as part of the Curonian colonisation. On 23 April 1816 Tumani Bojang, the King of Kombo, ceded it to Alexander Grant, the British commandant, in exchange for an annual fee of 103 iron bars. Grant's expedition of 75 men had been ordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maccarthy">Charles MacCarthy</a> to establish a military garrison. Grant founded a trading post and base to control the entrance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a> estuary and suppress the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>.</p><p>The British renamed the island St Mary's and called the town Bathurst, after the 3rd Earl Bathurst, Secretary of State for War and the Colonies. The streets were laid out in a modified grid and named after Allied generals at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Battle of Waterloo</a>.</p><p>Within a few years the town drew migrants: Africans of various origins, Levantines and Europeans. Most of its people were Muslim, with a significant Christian minority including the Aku. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolof">Wolof</a>, mainly from Gorée and Saint-Louis, were the largest African group, rising from 829 in 1881 to 10,130 in 1944. Islamic schools called dara were founded early, and the first Muslim court in 1905. Bathurst was declared capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a> in 1889, and became a centre of British activity in the colony. It was an important Allied naval and air hub in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, when its population rose from 14,370 in 1931 to 21,154 in 1944. After independence the town was renamed Banjul on 24 April 1973.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Banjul is the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a> and its economic and administrative centre, with the Central Bank of the Gambia and a port that ships groundnut products, beeswax, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> and hides. The city proper had 26,461 people at the 2024 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and the Greater Banjul Area, which includes the Kanifing municipality, 405,809. Arch 22, 35 metres tall, was built as an entrance portal to commemorate the bloodless coup of 22 July 1994 and houses a textile museum. The Gambian National Museum, the Albert Market and the State House are in the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Banjul” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1816 Captain Alexander Grant founded <b>Bathurst</b> on St Mary's Island at the mouth of the Gambia river, to suppress the slave trade and secure British trade after the loss of Goree.</blockquote><a href="/state/gambia-colony-and-protectorate">Gambia Colony and Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
